>
  <ContentNote title="Important limitation" tone="caution">
    <p>
      The Cooked Score is a proprietary educational estimate. It is not a
      standardized financial-health measure, credit score, lender decision,
      investment recommendation, or substitute for advice tailored to you.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <h2>What the score measures</h2>
  <p>
    Financial health is broader than a bank balance. Version 2.0.0 looks at four
    related questions that can be answered from information most people can
    find without building a complete financial plan:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cash buffer:</strong> how many months of essential spending your liquid cash could cover.</li>
    <li><strong>Monthly debt-payment load:</strong> how much of gross monthly income is already committed to debt payments.</li>
    <li><strong>Savings habit:</strong> how much of gross monthly income you regularly set aside.</li>
    <li><strong>Long-term progress:</strong> retirement assets relative to income and an age-based planning guidepost.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The result is designed to reveal which lever is currently helping most and
    which one may deserve attention next. It does not rank you against other
    people, and version 2.0.0 does not calculate a percentile or “financial age.”
  </p>

  <h2>The seven inputs</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Age</li>
    <li>Gross annual income, before tax and payroll deductions</li>
    <li>Essential monthly spending</li>
    <li>Liquid cash savings</li>
    <li>Total required monthly debt payments</li>
    <li>Recurring monthly savings and investment contributions</li>
    <li>Vested retirement and long-term investment account balances, including a current cash-balance pension value when one is stated</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    These inputs are deliberately narrower than a full financial plan. The
    score does not ask about taxes, dependants, insurance coverage, projected
    defined-benefit pension income, investment allocation, local living costs, debt interest rates,
    or near-term goals. Those omissions can materially change what a sensible
    next step looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not a credit score:</strong> it does not use payment history, credit utilization, credit-file age, or lender data.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a net-worth ranking:</strong> it does not estimate where you sit in an age percentile.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a retirement projection:</strong> it does not model returns, inflation, taxes, Social Security, projected defined-benefit pension income, or spending in retirement.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a diagnosis:</strong> the weights and thresholds are Cooked Finance design choices, not clinically validated standards.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use the result</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the four component scores before focusing on the total.</li>
    <li>Use one adult’s personal figures for every input and the same time period; run separate checks for partners.</li>
    <li>Choose one realistic action tied to the weakest useful component.</li>
    <li>Recheck quarterly, or after a substantial income, debt, or household change.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A higher score can indicate more room to absorb disruption under this
    model, but it never guarantees security. A lower score can reflect a
    temporary transition, caregiving, education, illness, unemployment, or a
    deliberate choice. Context belongs to you; the calculator cannot see it.
  </p>

